 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DY2AqEGu023/",
 "title": "How to Answer 'What is Your Biggest Strength'",
 "topic": "mindset",
 "summary": "The creator claims that answering interview questions about your biggest strength is not about sharing personality traits, but about solving the role's specific problems. She shares how framing her strength as resourcefulness helped her secure a job offer.",
 "key_points": [
  "Treat the question 'What is your biggest strength' as a job-fit question rather than a personality question.",
  "Solve the role's headache with your answer.",
  "For her role requiring daily coordination, the creator answered: 'I am resourceful. I always know someone who knows someone. So when work is stuck between teams, I move things forward.'",
  "For a client-facing role, the creator suggests saying: 'My strength is empathy and listening. I always understand the real problem behind the complaint before I respond.'",
  "For an execution-heavy role, the creator suggests saying: 'My strength is ownership. I close loops without waiting to be chased.'"
 ],
 "content": "This answer got me the offer [money emoji]. This answer got me the offer but most people get this question completely wrong. They asked me \"What is your biggest strength?\" Not hardworking. Not leadership. Not communication. I didn't say hard work or communication or leadership. I said I am resourceful. I always know someone who knows someone. So when work is stuck between teams, I move things forward. They came back after the interview and told me that answer is exactly why they were giving me the offer. That role required daily coordination between vendors and cross-functional teams and my answer solved the role's biggest problem. This is what most people get wrong. That question is not a personality question, it's a job-fit question. Your answer should solve their headache. Example, if it's a client-facing role, my strength is empathy and listening. I always understand the real problem behind the complaint before I respond. If it's an execution-heavy role, my strength is ownership. I close loops without waiting to be chased. I've put four more templates in the pinned comment. Check it out. Thank you.\n\nOn-screen text: This answer got me the offer [money emoji]. Not hardworking. Not leadership. Not communication. That answer is exactly why we're giving you this offer. The answer worked because it solved their exact problem [lightbulb emoji]. Template loading [right arrow emoji]. Client-facing [right arrow emoji] Empathy. Execution-heavy [right arrow emoji] Ownership. 4 more templates in the pinned comment [pointing down emoji].\n\nInstagram reel/post by CA Sruthilaya | Career & Money. Text / caption: They told me this answer is why I got the offer. Here’s the framework and how to adapt it to your exact role. #career #office #mindsetshift #interviewprep #interviewtips",
